  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Each letter from $a$ to $f$ stands for a different number from 1 to 6.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the largest integer value of the expression\n$$ (a\\times b)+\\frac{c}{d}-\\frac{e}{f}. $$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$31$.\n\nUse $a=5$, $b=6$, $c=3$, $d=1$, $e=4$, $f=2$.",
          "worked": "If $ab\\ne30$, then $ab\\le24$. Since $\\frac{c}{d}\\le6$ and $\\frac{e}{f}>0$, the expression is less than $30$.\n\nThus, any value greater than $30$ requires $a,b$ to be $5,6$, leaving $1,2,3,4$ for the fractions. Checking their distinct assignments, the greatest integer difference $\\frac{c}{d}-\\frac{e}{f}$ is $1$, achieved by $\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{4}{2}$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(5\\times6)+\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{4}{2}&=30+3-2\\\\(5\\times6)+\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{4}{2}&=31.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the largest integer value of the expression\n$$\\frac{a}{b}+\\frac{c}{d}+\\frac{e}{f}. $$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$9$.\n\nUse $a=6$, $b=3$, $c=5$, $d=1$, $e=4$, $f=2$.",
          "worked": "For an upper bound, put the denominators in increasing order. They are at least $1,2,3$. The greatest possible numerators in decreasing order are $6,5,4$. Pairing the largest numerator with the smallest denominator gives the greatest sum, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{a}{b}+\\frac{c}{d}+\\frac{e}{f}&\\le\\frac61+\\frac52+\\frac43\\\\\\frac{a}{b}+\\frac{c}{d}+\\frac{e}{f}&\\le\\frac{59}{6}<10.\\end{align*}$$\nThe largest possible integer is therefore at most $9$. It is achieved using all six numbers once:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac63+\\frac51+\\frac42&=2+5+2\\\\\\frac63+\\frac51+\\frac42&=9.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      }
    ]
  },
